Stick -> mouse motion: an optional response curve per mapping

InputPlumber 0.81.0 maps a stick to mouse motion linearly: a per-axis 0.20
deadzone, then speed_pps * deflection. That makes small pointer adjustments
hard. This adds an optional `curve` (exponent k) to a mapping's mouse motion
target. With it: a radial deadzone (0.20 of the vector magnitude), the
magnitude rescaled past it to m in [0, 1], and speed_pps * m^k with the
direction kept. Full deflection is still exactly speed_pps (the magnitude is
clamped to 1, so square-gate diagonals are not faster); k = 1 is linear.
Without it (or with a non-positive value) nothing changes: the per-axis
deadzone and linear speed as before. So only a profile that asks for it
gets the curve (HinterOS: its right-stick pointer for keyboard-profile
apps); other profiles that map a stick to the mouse keep upstream's feel.
The math is the pure function pointer_curve(), with unit tests.

Slow speeds stay smooth without a change: MouseDevice::poll already
accumulates the sub-pixel remainder (MouseMotionState::mouse_remainder) and
only emits whole-pixel REL_X/REL_Y deltas.

Not upstream: HinterOS-specific behaviour.

